Three steps from day rates to a price customers understand

Most consultants who turn their work into a product make the same first mistake: they price the new offer the way they priced their hours. A productized service is not a bundle of hours. It is a promise about an outcome, delivered the same way every time, and the price has to describe that promise.

When I moved my own practice from day rates to a fixed monthly service, I spent three weeks building a spreadsheet of my costs. It was useful, but it answered the wrong question. Customers never asked what it cost me. They asked what they would stop worrying about.

Start with the problem your best clients already pay to make disappear. Write it down in their words, not yours. Then list what you will deliver every month, what you will never deliver, and how quickly you will respond. That page is your product.

Write the promise before the price

Price anchors come next. Look at what the client spends today on the same problem: an internal hire, an agency retainer, the hours their founder loses each week. Your price should sit comfortably below the cheapest serious alternative while still leaving you a healthy margin.

Finally, publish the price. Founders who hide prices behind a call are optimizing for the deals they lose, not the ones they win. A visible price filters out the wrong customers before they ever book time with you, and it tells the right ones that you have done this before.

I raised my price twice in the first year. Both times, the number of new customers barely moved, and churn went down. The customers who stayed were the ones who valued the outcome, which is exactly who a productized service is for.

